speechtext:
I think the discussion has taken a very wide scope. I do not think that any responsibility rests on the agent; the whole responsibility in practice falls on the merchant. As a merchant I go to the United States or to England and buy what I consider a good fertilizer. When I bring that to Canada and sell it to my patrons I am responsible for that brand. It is very unlikely that a manufacturer will

send an agent here to sell his product; the man who imports direct is more likely to buy direct from the manufacturer so as to get the article at a lower price. I think the man importing is more reliable than the agent. If he sells a fraudulent fertilizer this year, where will he be next year? I am rather astonished that hon. gentlemen think manufacturers will commit frauds. That has not been my experience;
I have very seldom been caught by an agent. I have generally dealt with houses that when a complaint was made to them would rectify it.
